        "prompt": "You are the Standards axis of a two-axis code review. Review ONLY for coding standards and code smells, not spec correctness.\n\nContext:\n- Fixed point: main (commit 1e23395 \"Initial fixture: add() has a subtraction bug\"). Current branch is main with one uncommitted change.\n- Diff command for the change under review: `git diff HEAD -- calc.py` (working tree vs HEAD; `git diff main...HEAD` is empty because HEAD == main).\n- The diff:\n```\ndiff --git a/calc.py b/calc.py\nindex 95ab23e..61919ce 100644\n--- a/calc.py\n+++ b/calc.py\n@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@\n def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n     \"\"\"Return the sum of a and b.\"\"\"\n-    return a - b\n+    return a + b\n```\n- Commit list: `git log main..HEAD --oneline` is empty (no new commits yet); the change is uncommitted.\n- Standards-source files found: NONE. No CODING_STANDARDS.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, or other documented standards exist in the repo (only docs/agents/issue-tracker.md, which is about the issue workflow, not code style).\n\nSmell baseline (applies even with no documented standards; repo standards would override it, but there are none; each smell is a judgement call, never a hard violation; skip anything tooling enforces):\n- Mysterious Name: a function, variable, or type whose name doesn't reveal what it does or holds. \u2192 rename it; if no honest name comes, the design's murky.\n- Duplicated Code: the same logic shape appears in more than one hunk or file in the change. \u2192 extract the shared shape, call it from both.\n- Feature Envy: a method that reaches into another object's data more than its own. \u2192 move the method onto the data it envies.\n- Data Clumps: the same few fields or params keep travelling together (a type wanting to be born). \u2192 bundle them into one type, pass that.\n- Primitive Obsession: a primitive or string standing in for a domain concept that deserves its own type. \u2192 give the concept its own small type.\n- Repeated Switches: the same switch/if